//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): reserve pages and give them straight back.
//
// `give` accepts only <!filled>, and <filled!> is minted only by `write`, `read`
// or the named escape `untouched`. So reserving two pages and releasing them
// without ever touching them is a program you cannot write.
//
// The asymmetry matters MORE at page granularity than at byte granularity. A
// two-page reservation is 8 KiB out of a unikernel's 2 MB RAM floor — 0.4% of
// the machine, taken and returned with no work done. The same shape in bytes is
// 64 bytes and a shrug. If the gate is worth having anywhere, it is worth having
// here, and the escape is worth being able to grep for here most of all:
//
//     grep -r "pages:untouched"
//
// The C has nothing to say about it. `uk_palloc(a, 2)` followed by
// `uk_pfree(a, p, 2)` is correct, warning-free and does no work. There is no
// `UK_ASSERT` in ukalloc that objects, and there could not be — the allocator
// cannot know whether the caller read the region.
//
// The legitimate case stays expressible and stays loud: a guard region, or a run
// held so a later `palloc` cannot take it, says `unikraft/pages:untouched` and
// compiles. What this rejects is the SILENT no-op.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch: expected
//           'unikraft.pages:filled' but got 'unikraft.pages:blank!'
//           for argument 'pages'

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/pages
~import std/io

~unikraft/pages:default
| ready pager |> unikraft/pages:take(pager, count: 2)
    | pages region |> unikraft/pages:give(pages: region)
        |> std/io:print.ln("should never get here")
    | exhausted e |> std/io:print.ln("take({{ e.count:d }}) failed: {{ e.reason:s }}")
| unavailable why |> std/io:print.ln("no page allocator: {{ why:s }}")
